From 14fe14833db6705e56c2f121b2c722238f6c39d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sim <sim@example.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 15:05:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Raise parallel connection cap to 64 (native tier) with
 capacity & memory guards

Config chain: MAX_PARALLEL_CONNECTION_COUNT_NATIVE_MEMORY=64 as single
source of truth (read clamp, both write clamps, UI slider).
Capacity: executor 32->80 core, connection pool idle 32->128,
dispatcher 64/32->128/128 (defensive; sync calls bypass it).
Memory: depth floor conn+1 (was forced 2x conn, overcommitting and
crashing coerceIn at 64), engine-side chunk guard on the native tier.
Retry: chunk budget 2->4 attempts, jittered 50-80ms pause.
UA: honest versioned player identity on the playback chain (was
desktop-Chrome string contradicting TLS fingerprint + Range behavior).
429 fuse, Retry-After, warm session, eviction guard untouched.

Chunk-budget fix (nativeChunkBudgetMb): the guard, the prefetch depth
and the UI chunk filter charged the whole allocator target as the
reserve (safeNative - targetBufferSizeMb). In managed performance mode
the target EQUALS the safe native limit, so the budget collapsed to 0:
every connection count pinned to the guard's 1 MB floor (settings UI
offered only 256 KB / 512 KB / 1 MB) and the depth check went inert at
its c+1 floor. The window lives INSIDE the target (buildLoadControl
subtracts it, streaming floored at MIN_BUFFER_MB), so the budget is
min(target, safeNative) - MIN_BUFFER_MB via MemoryBudget.nativeChunkBudgetMb,
wired at all three sites. Ledger tests pin the real RAM tiers
(100/200/500/1000/1600/2000) in MemoryBudgetNativeChunkBudgetTest.
